                <pb facs="0631" n="623" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg623"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/6469: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Hong Kong</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Donovan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hong
                                Kong</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-09-28T12:00:00+08:00">September 28,
                        1937—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received September 28—6:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Referring to my telegram of September 8, 3 p.m.,<anchor n="7" xml:id="d779-f1"/>
                    Chinese Government is removing its stocks of silver stored at Hong Kong to
                    London beginning with the shipment of $25,000,000 national currency last week
                    for following reasons: fear that a declaration of war by Japan would make the
                    transfer of the silver from Hong Kong difficult if not impossible since Japanese
                    naval vessels might not hesitate to remove it from British vessels, upon which
                    all Chinese Government silver destined for England is shipped; if stored in
                    London silver stocks can be used for credit and financial purposes but can not
                    be so used in Hong Kong. Shipments expected to continue at rate of about
                    $25,000,000 per week.</p>
                <pb facs="0632" n="624" xml:id="pg624"/>
                <p>Information furnished by Hong Kong and Shanghai Banking Corporation.</p>
                <p>Nanking not informed since only wireless communication available.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Donovan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
